/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281215 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186280267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281215 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "db5c5640-bf67-5d54-8096-feeae76158b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281274 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281274 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281274 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186280554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281274 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f71ef8c6-7ff1-5162-aa30-762dd2ff7e93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281493 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281493 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7ca9996f6d0185d99752ccf5201b5300e0e5149ad674bb9676cf8c716b71a171.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9dd906e8-cb51-59ae-a963-a23ed28e4195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281493 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](images/32/320e0c3333aa59f4e856d7d869d6fece09b178ac1abb3acd402621977df9b496.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2(3)\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{3}{(25-{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-4\\pi (64-125) \\\\  & =-4\\pi (-61) \\\\  & =244\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,2(0)\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =244\\pi +0 \\\\  & =244\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281495 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281495 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281495 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/de/dedd58b5eeeba8b2874c740543132026f95157597dd12ad31fbaa476a3d77c2e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6aee075a-fd15-5236-b83e-02176157c22a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281495 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](images/9f/9ffcdd701a8ac6b12cd62773e1cabfd2ce95f9a809e18d087c4bf4f4a22eadb5.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,({4}^{2})(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{r}^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(6-{r}^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[3{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =32\\pi (\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,{0}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =216\\pi +0 \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281497 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/04/04f58e972500033523e3ca3247ea4e1c5c377d8dc2b402b1c0200d9c85529894.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ad458af-f12d-5b37-8e06-6096fcdae441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281497 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](images/86/8652a197f603c517cd462c69473e3a693ba8dcd76340b6241f890663356e00cb.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6{x}^{2}+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{x}^{3}+2xz]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16+4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[16z+2{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-2{∬}_{D}z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2\\cdot [x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =18+(-2) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281498 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281498 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281498 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281498 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/63/638a02dec15c2cecd8563854c2fb071ff70f10004f60339ef92d0914e7198ebc.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bdcab013-1b4f-5535-a84d-7952ce7d745b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281498 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/4f/4fd228a9bf0a2d8382139d3f804ee7c9b3fd8721c8ad079de43e61b9e8947240.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=1$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =4+3{y}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(4+2z)y+{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+2z+1\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2z+5\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[{z}^{2}+5z]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-(3{y}^{2}+2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(3{y}^{2}+2z)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{y}^{3}+2yz]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[z+{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-(3+9) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =24+(-12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423369 #edb/ref 1080 "-9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423369 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423369 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06134337-3698-5e99-b304-3c524f8d58ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423370 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423370 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423370 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08051b4d-e523-5380-b21a-12e9c0665d2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423373 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423373 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423373 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ce314fb-b190-50fc-b3d0-c8253bbb95a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423375 #edb/ref 1080 "16\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423375 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423375 