7592186045417] [17592186423436 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423436 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "919ef9fd-60f6-5c91-983a-1dea7afd83ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423437 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423437 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423437 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423437 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a4c323a0-5a8f-5466-a1d8-57f7d5162590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d3/d333ab14f8a42bb1d40894421afc76ff60acbb6c4cb858ddee5f6e8a39be2d15.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $1$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0aa0eff9-8245-5a13-9648-df63821931fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/51/5193451b39b03b11cf9780fd7039360ba8fb5de5876a3593f7a5dadde2e7be5b.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-1}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-0}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+(-8\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e8/e8708a16a0f6db347acf2c1a82a602d2fa8ce60d4bb8369f1414eca3465e4e71.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b265113a-f521-5f72-933b-1132d7a6f831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/8e/8ed6ffdeab54bc6888d245ef4f1c050c31e010c67dfa93aea365fc6cb523e382.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy-4x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y-3\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-3y]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}2x+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+(-\\frac{15}{2}) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e73d17583a6aa15601abd957f25cfb11ded37159649a67c5b237915d03ed98f7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $2$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6c92f89-f1d7-5cd5-9f3c-fab3133854ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x(x-3z)+{y}^{2}+z(z+3z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-3xz+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e6/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3x+4y,\\,3y-4x,\\,3z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cea56a0f-1b32-5284-84a0-25dd8320e070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x+4y,\\,3y-4x,\\,3z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =x(3x+4y)+y(3y-4x)+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+4xy+3{y}^{2}-4xy+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =12\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186476000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1045 "Flux Through Closed Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref